=== QT001 | a literal dictionary whose sides differ in length
f:{[] `a`b!1 2 3}

=== clean | a literal dictionary that balances
f:{[] `a`b!1 2}

=== clean | keys and values built at runtime are not a literal
f:{[k;v] k!v}

=== QT002 | a symbol passed to a function that is visibly numeric
f:{[r] r+1};f[`bad]

=== clean | the same function given a number
f:{[r] r+1};f[2]

=== QT003 | a symbol under arithmetic is a runtime type error
2+`a

=== QT003 | either side, and vectors likewise
1 2+`a`b

=== clean | the symbols on the right are the start of a find, which returns a long
r:2*`a`b?`b

=== clean | nor a dictionary built from them
r:1+`a`b!1 2

=== clean | nor a match, which is a boolean
r:1+`a~`b

=== clean | a symbol ending one line and a print opening the next are two statements
y:`c`c`c`j
-1"next";

=== QT003 | a symbol on the left is the operand whatever follows
r:`a+`b?`c

=== clean | a char meets a float, or sits right of minus, or is divided: q 5 runs all three
r1:1.5+"a"
r2:"ab"%2
r3:2-"x"

=== QT023 | but integer arithmetic on a char is 'type on q 5
r:"a"*3

=== QT023 | a char left of minus too
r:"a"-1

=== QT023 | and through a name assigned a string
a:"abc"
b:a*2

=== clean | a name of the same kind hidden by a parameter is not the string
a:"x"
f:{[a] a*2}

=== clean | take builds symbol vectors without arithmetic
2#`a`b`c

=== QT001 | the value scan stops at the end of the line, as q does
d:`a`b!1 2 3

=== clean | the same dictionary balanced, with no semicolon after it
d:`a`b!1 2

=== QT004 | a symbol-named cast on a string converts char codes
n:`long$"123"

=== QT004 | the date cast is ten dates, not one
d:`date$"2024.01.01"

=== QT032 profile: general | symbol and guid by name refuse a string (q 5: `symbol$"abc" and `guid$"abc" are 'type)
s:`symbol$"abc"
g:`guid$"abc"

=== clean profile: general | the casts that convert char codes run, so they are style (q 5: `long$"12" is 49 50)
n:`long$"12"

=== clean | the uppercase-char cast parses text
n:"J"$"123"

=== clean | the bare-backtick cast makes a symbol
s:`$"abc"

=== clean | char and byte are legitimate on a string
c:`char$"ab";b:`byte$"ab"

=== clean | a cast on a non-string
n:`long$1.5

=== QT005 | every column scalar is a rank error
t:([] a:1; b:`x)

=== QT005 | keyed too
t:([k:1] v:2)

=== clean | one vector column extends the rest
t:([] a:1; b:2 3)

=== clean | enlist makes the row
t:([] a:enlist 1)

=== clean | a string column is a vector
t:([] a:"ab")

=== clean | an empty column
t:([] a:())

=== QT006 | mismatched literal vectors under plus
r:1 2 3+4 5

=== QT006 | mismatched symbol vectors under equals
r:`a`b=`a`b`c

=== QT006 | minus with spaces on both sides is the operator
r:1 2 3 - 1 2

=== clean | minus with a space before and none after is a negative literal
r:1 2 3 -1 2

=== clean | a negative exponent is part of the float, not an infix minus
r:0.01 0.558313 9e-08 0.3 0.4 0.5

=== clean | nor is a positive one
r:1.5e+10 2.5e-3 3e4

=== clean | scientific notation either side of an infix, at equal lengths
r:1e-3 2e-3=3e-3 4e-3

=== QT006 | a mismatch is still a mismatch when an operand uses an exponent
r:1 2 9e-08+4 5

=== clean | the right operand runs on past a join, so its length is not the literal's
r:1 2 3=4 5,6

=== clean | nor when the join is spaced
r:`a`b`c=`a`b , `c

=== QT006 | a mismatched right operand that ends the statement
r:1 2 3=4 5;

=== clean | same length
r:1 2 3+4 5 6

=== clean | a scalar extends
r:1 2 3+4

=== clean | join takes any lengths
r:1 2,3 4 5

=== QT007 | ssr on a symbol
r:ssr[`abc;"a";"b"]

=== QT007 | a symbol as the pattern
r:ssr["abc";`a;"b"]

=== QT007 | infix ss on a symbol
r:`abc ss "a"

=== clean | ssr on a string
r:ssr["abc";"a";"b"]

=== clean | trim and lower accept symbols
r:trim `a;s:lower `A

=== clean | a cast to string is not a symbol literal
r:ss[`char$x;"a"]

=== QA011 | a boolean vector cannot be a conditional's test
r:$[101b;1;2]

=== QA011 | nor a numeric vector
r:$[1 2 3;1;2]

=== QA011 | nor a symbol
r:$[`a;1;2]

=== QA011 | a comparison against a vector literal is a vector too
r:$[(1 2 3)>2;`y;`n]

=== QA011 | with the vector on either side
r:$[x>1 2 3;1;2]

=== clean | any, match and in reduce to an atom
r1:$[any x>1 2 3;1;2]
r2:$[x~1 2;1;2]
r3:$[x in 1 2 3;1;2]

=== clean | an atom is what $ wants
r:$[1b;1;2]

=== clean | and a long atom counts
r:$[1;1;2]

=== QT015 QB018 | a symbol against a number is a type error, and both sides are literals
r:1=`a

=== QT015 | and against a string
r:"abc"=`abc

=== QT015 QB018 | ordering comparisons too, and again both are literals
r:`x<1

=== QB018 | symbol against symbol is not a type error, but it is still two literals
r:`a=`b

=== QB018 | a char compares by its code, which q allows - though two literals are still constant
r:1="a"

=== clean | match never raises, whatever the types
r:1~`a

=== clean | a symbol naming a cast is not an operand
r:$[0i=`int$period;1;2]

=== clean | 0! unkeys a table; the 0 is not the operand
r:`time in 0!t

=== QT008 | til wants an integer
r:til[1.5]

=== clean | an integer is what it wants
r:til[5]

=== QT009 | where wants booleans or counts
r:where[1.5 2.5]

=== clean | booleans are the usual argument
r:where[101b]

=== QT010 | a numeric aggregate over symbols
r:sum[`a`b]

=== clean | the same aggregate over numbers
r:sum[1 2 3]

=== QT011 | sorting an atom sorts nothing
r:asc[1]

=== clean | a list is what asc is for
r:asc[3 1 2]

=== QT012 | distinct of an atom likewise
r:distinct[1]

=== clean | a list again
r:distinct[1 1 2]

=== QT013 | flip wants something with two dimensions
r:flip[1 2]

=== clean | a dictionary of lists has them
r:flip[`a`b!(1 2;3 4)]

=== QT014 | a rotate count must be an integer atom
r:rotate[1.5;1 2]

=== clean | an integer count
r:rotate[1;1 2 3]

=== QT020 | vector columns of different lengths
t:([]a:1 2;b:3 4 5)

=== QT020 | enlist makes a one-item vector, which does not extend
t:([]a:enlist 1;b:2 3)

=== QT020 | on the key side too
t:([k:1 2]v:3 4 5)

=== clean | an atom beside a vector extends to its length
t:([]a:1;b:2 3)

=== clean | equal lengths
t:([]a:1 2;b:3 4)

=== clean | a boolean vector is a vector: two bits, two rows
t:([]a:10b;b:1)

=== clean | a byte vector likewise
t:([]a:0x0102;b:1)

=== QT005 | one bit is a boolean atom, so every column here is an atom
t:([]a:1b;b:1)

=== QT005 | a keyed table needs vectors on both sides
t:([k:1]v:2 3)

=== QT005 | a single char is an atom, whatever the quotes suggest
t:([]a:"a";b:1)

=== clean | a two-char string is a two-row column
t:([]a:"ab";b:1)

=== QT021 | ss with an empty pattern
r:ss["abc";""]

=== QT021 | ssr likewise
r:ssr["abc";"";"b"]

=== clean | a pattern with something in it
r:ss["abc";"a"]

=== QT008 | til with a float, written by juxtaposition - the spelling real q uses
r:til 1.5

=== QT010 | a numeric aggregate over symbols, juxtaposed
r:sum `a`b

=== QT011 | sorting an atom, juxtaposed
r:asc 1

=== clean | a name as the argument says nothing about its type
r:asc x

=== clean | the literal is not the whole argument: q reads right to left, so this is til of a product
r:til 1.5*x

=== clean | nor here, where the vector is joined to something before sum sees it
r:sum 1 2 3,x

=== QT006 | a byte vector has one item per pair of hex digits
r:1 2 3+0x0102

=== QT006 | a parenthesised list has one item per top-level semicolon plus one
r:(1;2;3)+4 5

=== clean | the same shapes at matching lengths
r:1 2+0x0102

=== QT001 | a null or an infinity is an item like any other (q 5: `a`b`c!0N 0N is 'length)
d:`a`b`c!0N 0N
e:`a`b!0N 0N 0N

=== QT006 | under an operator too (q 5: 1 2=0n 1 2 and 0W 1+1 2 3 are 'length)
r:1 2=0n 1 2
s:0W 1+1 2 3
t:1 2 3*-0w 1

=== QT001 QT006 | applied with brackets (q 5: +[0N 1;1 2 3] and ![`a`b;0N 1 2] are 'length)
r:+[0N 1;1 2 3]
d:![`a`b;0N 1 2]

=== clean | nulls and infinities at matching lengths (q 5: each runs)
d:`a`b!0N 0N
r:1 2 3=0n 1 2
s:0W 1+1 2
t:0N+1 2 3

=== QT001 | the keys need not be symbols: a parenthesised list
d:(1;2;3)!1 2

=== QT001 | or a numeric vector
d:1 2!3 4 5

=== clean | an atom on the left keys or unkeys a table instead
k:1!([]a:1 2;b:3 4)

=== clean | numeric keys and values of one length
d:1 2!3 4

=== QT001 | a dictionary's values as a parenthesised list, one short
d:`a`b!(1;2;3)

=== QT015 | a number tested for membership in symbols
r:1 in `a`b

=== QT015 | and a symbol in numbers
r:`a in 1 2

=== clean | the number is the start of a longer right operand: sublist builds symbols
r:`a in 2 sublist `a`b`c

=== clean | nor take, with spaces around it
r:`a in 1 # `a`b

=== clean | find returns longs, so the symbols are not the operand
r:1=`a`b?`b

=== QT015 | a symbol against a numeric vector that ends the statement
r:`a=1 2;

=== clean | a comparison inside a comment is not code
x:1 / "a"=`b

=== clean | the quotes between two strings do not make a third
x:("";"=`b")

=== QT015 | a string against a symbol, followed by a comment
r:"abc"=`abc / note

=== clean | symbol membership in symbols is what in is for
r:`a in `a`b

=== QT022 | a name assigned an atom, then indexed
a:1
r:a[0]

=== QT022 | with nothing in the brackets too
a:1.5
r:a[]

=== clean | a symbol atom indexed looks up the variable it names, which is not this
a:`x
r:a[0]

=== clean | a name assigned twice is not known
a:1
a:2 3
r:a[0]

=== clean | a parameter of the same name hides the global
a:1
f:{[a] a[0]}

=== QT006 | named vectors of different lengths
a:1 2 3
b:1 2
r:a+b

=== QT006 | a named vector against a literal one
a:1 2 3
r:a+1 2

=== clean | join and match take any lengths, and an atom extends
a:1 2 3
b:1 2
r1:a,b
r2:a~b
r3:a+1

=== clean | the right operand runs on, so its length is not b's
a:1 2 3
b:1 2
r:a+b,0

=== clean | brackets with nothing after them are q 5's dictionary literal, not a table
pkg:([arch:"expat"; ubuntu:"libexpat1"; centos:""])
env:([a:1;b:2 3])


=== clean | a boolean vector is a digit per item (q 5: `a`b!10b is a dictionary)
`a`b!10b

=== QT001 | and still counted against the keys (q 5: `a`b`c!10b is 'length)
`a`b`c!10b

=== clean | one symbol on the left is an enumeration (q 5: `sym!0 1 2 is type 20h)
sym:`a`b`c
x:`sym!0 1 2

=== clean | set in brackets rebinds the name too (q 5: a:1;set[`a;2 3];a 0 is 2)
